{"name":"Tiki Hut","hex":"#8a6e45","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tiki","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A carved talisman in humanoid form, common to the cultures of the Pacific Ocean.","The characteristic style of tiki bars."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tiki"},{"term":"hut","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, simple one-storey dwelling or shelter, often with just one room, and generally built of readily available local materials.","A small wooden shed."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide (someone) with shelter in a hut.","To take shelter in a hut."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tiki","blocks":[{"text":"In Māori mythology, Tiki is the first man created by either Tūmatauenga or Tāne. He found the first woman, Marikoriko, in a pond; she seduced him, and he became the father of Hine-kau-ataata. By extension, a tiki is a large or small wooden, pounamu or other stone carving in humanoid form, although this is a somewhat archaic usage in the Māori language, where a tiki is usually a hei-tiki, a pendant worn around the neck. Hei-tiki are often considered taonga, especially if they are older and have been passed down throughout multiple generations. Carvings similar to tiki and coming to represent deified ancestors are found in most Polynesian cultures. They often serve to mark the boundaries of sacred or significant sites. The word has cognates in other Polynesian languages, such as tiʻi in Tahitian and kiʻi in Hawaiian. In the Western world, Tiki culture, a movement inspired by various Pacific cultures, has become popular in the 20th and 21st centuries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Tiki1905.jpg/330px-Tiki1905.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiki","wikidata":"Q13389032"},{"title":"Hut","blocks":[{"text":"A hut is a small dwelling, which may be constructed of various local materials. Huts are a type of vernacular (non-academic) architecture, being built of readily available materials like mud."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Chozos_de_ganaderos%2C_El_Torno%2C_C%C3%A1ceres.jpg/330px-Chozos_de_ganaderos%2C_El_Torno%2C_C%C3%A1ceres.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hut","wikidata":"Q5784097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg/500px-Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg/500px-Kii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hawaiian kiʻi at Puʻuhonua o Hōnaunau National Historical Park","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKii_at_Puuhonua_O_Honaunau_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg/500px-Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg/1280px-Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg/500px-Tiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tiki statuette from the Marquesas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATiki_Marquesas_Louvre_MH_87-50-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Tiki_statue_shop_2%2C_Hawaii%2C_1959.jpg/500px-Tiki_statue_shop_2%2C_Hawaii%2C_19